# CoreCLR Interpreter Hot-Update Conventions

## Project Layout

| Directory | Role |
|-----------|------|
| `CoreClrInterpTest/` | Standalone interpreter benchmark app |
| `BenchLib/` | R2R-compiled benchmark library for Mode 1 native comparison |
| `NativeHost/` | C++ host embedding CoreCLR via hostfxr |
| `NativeHost/managed/` | C# plugin loaded by the C++ host |
| `LuaHost/` | C++ Lua 5.4 host for performance comparison |
| `IlScanner/` | Mono.Cecil-based IL security scanner |
| `TestPluginSafe/`, `TestPluginDangerous/` | Scanner test fixtures |
| `ILRuntimeSmokeTest/` | Legacy ILRuntime third-party interpreter tests |
| `Wasi/` | WASI-WASM interpreter baseline |

## Key Conventions

- Target framework is `net11.0` for all CoreCLR interpreter projects.
- C++ projects use CMake with `/MT` static CRT to match `libnethost.lib`.
- C# exports to C++ use `[UnmanagedCallersOnly]` — no COM, no P/Invoke wrappers.
- C# → C++ callbacks use `delegate* unmanaged[Stdcall]<...>` function pointers received from native `Init`.
- `runtimeconfig.json` is maintained manually alongside plugin csproj (required by hostfxr).

## Interpreter Testing

- Custom CoreCLR with interpreter enabled built from `F:\runtime` (modified `clrfeatures.cmake` and `clr.featuredefines.props`).
- Use `corerun.exe` with `DOTNET_InterpMode=1` and `CORE_LIBRARIES` pointing to installed shared framework for quick testing.
- For hostfxr-based testing, use `dotnet_interp/` custom runtime root with matching `coreclr.dll`, `clrinterpreter.dll`, `clrjit.dll`, `System.Private.CoreLib.dll`.

## IL Scanning Rules

- Scanner blocklist defined in `IlScanner/ScanPolicy.cs` — extend `GameEditorDefault` for new dangerous APIs.
- Scanner checks: namespace/type/method blocklist, P/Invoke, `calli`, unsafe pointers, explicit layout.
- Test both safe and dangerous plugins after policy changes.

## String Formatting in ILRuntime

ILRuntime does not support `ref struct` (`DefaultInterpolatedStringHandler`) or `InlineArray` (used by `string.Format` args). In code that may run under ILRuntime, use manual `.ToString()` + string concatenation instead.
